    \u043e\u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0434\u0438\u043b\u0438 \u0441\u0432\u043e\u0451 \u0432\u0440\u0435\u043c\u044f."
  interestingFactsEn: "Bhaskara II was certainly a brilliant scientist, but his personality and works are wrapped up in many fascinating and unusual stories, and these are the most interesting facts that show him not only as a mathematician, but also as an engineer, a poet, and a man who has passed the time.\n\nThe designer of the Eternal Engine may be the most surprising fact: Bhaskara is one of the first ever to produce the Eternal Engine (perpetrum mobile) projects. In his poem around 1150 he described a wheel with tilted receptacles, partly filled with mercury, on its rim, which was designed to transfer mercury into the receptacles, creating a permanent imbalance and forcing the wheel to spin forever.Although, from a physical point of view, this mechanism could not work because of friction, the idea and analysis of moments of gravity for its time were revolutionary, and it was from the wheel of Bhaskara that a centuries-old history of trying to create an eternal engine that inspired even Leonardo da Vinci.\n\nThe Bhaskara challenge in 500 years has developed a cyclical method called \"Chakrava\" (which in the Sanskrit translation means \"coleso\" in the Cyclic nature of the algorithm) to solve the most complex, uncertain equations, in particular the Pelle equation (\\(x×2 = 1 + py·2}).He found a huge whole solution for the case of \\(p=61 ): \\(x = 1.766.319.049 \\) \\(y = 226,153.980 \\) The most striking: five centuries later, in 1657, French mathematician Pierre Ferma proposed the same task (\\(xxx2 = 61y2 + 1 \\)) as a challenge to his colleagues in Europe. European mathematicians did not solve it until half a century, and their methods (chain fractions) were much more difficult than the elegant Bhaskara method.Herman Gankel, a famous mathematician historian, called the \"Chakrava\" method \"the best achieved in the number theory before Lagranga\".\n\nLilawati: Mathematics, as comfort, is the most touching and romantic fact related to his famous arithmetic work, \"Lilavati\", which translates as \"Pretty\" or \"Giggling.\" According to legend, Bhaskara named a book after his daughter Lilavati. He figured out that a happy hour for her wedding would come at a fixed time.To avoid it, he made a water watch — a cup with a hole in the water vessel that was supposed to be sunk at the right moment. But the curious bride came to the instrument, and the pearl of her ornament fell into the water and sealed the hole. The good hour was missed, and the prediction came true -- she was unable to marry or became a widow. To comfort her daughter, Bhaskara wrote a book on mathematics for her, and forever her name was dead.It's important to note: historians are cautious about this legend; maybe Lilavati was a wife or student, or maybe it's a whole literary act, a poetic appeal to an imaginary student to make a boring textbook more alive.\n\n* Mathematics in poems and tasks for a beautiful woman, whether Lilavati was a real person, Bhaskara did write his mathematical tasks in a poem form and often turned to them as \"a beautiful girl\".For example, in Lilavati, there are tasks like, \"Oh, Lilawati, smart girl, if you understand addition and subtract, tell me the sum of numbers 2, 5, 32, 193, 18, 10 and 100, and the result of subtracting them from 10,000.\" \"A child with deer eyes, tell me what 135 times 12 is going to be, if you understand multiplication by bit and by bit.\" And here's one of the poetic tasks on the equation systems: > By making love, the necklace broke.> A number of pearls fell. > The sixth part fell to the floor. > The fifth part fell to the bed. > The young woman saved a third. > The tenth part was picked up by her lover. > If there were six pearls left on the thread, > How many pearls was there? These tasks not only entertained but also taught to solve complex mathematical concepts through living, understandable images.\n\nThe first traveler to work with the infinity of Bhaskar was one of the first to understand the concept of infinity, which involved a zero division. He made it clear that the value divided by zero becomes infinity.Despite this mistake, the very fact that he was trying to understand this mathematical abstraction in the 12th century suggests his genius.\n\n♪ The anticipation of differential calculation many historians believe that Bhaskara came to the ideas that led to Newton and Leibnitz's differential calculation in Europe. His astronomical work has an understanding of the principle of differential. For example, he knew that \\(delta \\sin x \\approx \\cos, \\delta x \\) and that at the point of the extreme function, her differential coefficient turns to zero.He didn't create a complete theory, but these intuitive guesses are five centuries ahead of their time."
